         <div type="letter">
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <p>
                        <name type="person" key="joyc82">
                            <choice>
                                <sic>Gimmi</sic>
                                <corr>Jimmy</corr>
                            </choice>
                        </name> e <name type="person" key="joyc05">
                            <choice>
                                <sic>Geoces</sic>
                                <corr>George</corr>
                            </choice>
                        </name> partiti arrivano <name type="geo" key="G_ITA_DESE">Desenzano</name> 9 ore</p>
                    <closer>
                        <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">
                            <name type="person" key="barn84">Nora <choice>
                                    <sic>Geoces</sic>
                                    <corr>Joyce</corr>
                                </choice>
                            </name>
                        </seg>
                    </closer>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="global-note1" type="global">According to dates and times stamped on this telegram, it was sent by “Nora Geoces” (Nora Barnacle Joyce) from Trieste on 8 June 1920 at “12.10” (p.m.), and was received in Sirmione on the same day at “16.10” (4.10 p.m.).</note>
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">“Jimmy and George departed arrive Desenzano 9 o’clock. Nora Joyce.” After various setbacks, real, imagined, and contrived, JJ arrived at Desenzano, the railway stop a few miles from Sirmione, with his son Giorgio, on the evening of 8 June 1920, and spent two days with Pound and his wife Dorothy in Sirmione (for full accounts, see <rs type="letter" key="L_68605">JJ telegrams to Pound, 26 May</rs> and <rs type="letter" key="L_68612">5 June 1920</rs>, notes; <rs type="letter" key="L_68611">JJ letter to Pound, 5 June 1920</rs>, notes). Whether “9 ore” should be translated as “9 o’clock” or “nine hours” is really immaterial. Nora sent the telegram a few minutes after noon, so JJ and Giorgio would have arrived in Desenzano in nine hours, or at roughly 9 p.m., barring delays. Pound had reckoned that the train from Trieste to Desenzano would take about nine hours (<name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Pound/Joyce</name> 166). JJ estimated – with Circean exaggeration and in one of his moods to stay put – that the Trieste-Desenzano train, starting at 11:30 a.m., would arrive “about the witching hour of midnight” (<name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Pound/Joyce</name> 167). In any event, JJ and son made it to the Desenzano train station on the night of 8 June 1920, where Pound met them and took them to the Hotel Eden in Sirmione, where he and Dorothy were staying. (Some years later, JJ wrote Harriet Shaw Weaver of having met Pound at Desenzano and driving with him “across the country by night” [<name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">SL</name> 319].)</note>
            </div>
